{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcSyHcMaDyYrDgBLogkG", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I haven't changed anything yet. A naive retry here could charge customers twice, so I'd like you to pick an approach first.\n\n**The problem:** the project has a recorded hazard note saying `finalize()` sends the invoice to the external ledger without an idempotency key (a request ID that lets the ledger spot duplicates). Calling it twice records the invoice twice, and the note says never to wrap it in retries. The code agrees: `src/billing/invoices.py:22` is a plain `requests.post` with no request ID.\n\nThe risky case is a read timeout or a connection that drops after the ledger has already recorded the invoice. We see an error, retry, and the invoice is recorded a second time. `finalize_once()` doesn't protect against this because `ledger_ref` is only set after a successful response.\n\n**Options, safest first:**\n\n1. **Add an idempotency key, then retry freely.** If the ledger API accepts something like an `Idempotency-Key` header, we send a stable key per invoice (e.g. `invoice.id`). Then up to 3 retries on any network error is safe. This is the right fix, but it depends on what the ledger supports. Do you know if it does?\n2. **Retry only when the request clearly never reached the ledger.** Retry up to 3 times on `requests.exceptions.ConnectTimeout` and on connection-refused errors (failures while connecting). Read timeouts and mid-response drops would still fail, because the invoice may already be recorded. This is safe today and probably covers most of the failures you're seeing, but not all of them.\n3. **Check, then retry.** After an unclear failure, ask the ledger whether this invoice was already recorded before posting again. This needs a lookup endpoint on the ledger.\n\nI'd suggest option 1 if the ledger supports it, and option 2 otherwise. Which do you want?"}], "container": 